          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Adjoint functor theorems
            are theorems stating that under certain conditions a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            that preserves
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
          %p
            A basic result of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is that right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserve
            all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            that exist in their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/domain"} domain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %em adjoint functor theorem
            is a statement that (under certain conditions) the converse holds: a functor which preserves limits is a right adjoint.
          %p
            The basic idea of an adjoint functor theorem is that
            %em if
            we could assume that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            had all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrams"} diagrams
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R : D \to C
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
            all these limits we might define its would-be left adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            by taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L c
            to be the limit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi c
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi d
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L c \coloneqq \lim_{c\to R d} d
          %p
            This notation stands for a limit over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c/R
            (whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pairs"} pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi d
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d,f:c\to R d)
            and whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
            are arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d\to d'
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            making the obvious triangle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commute
            in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi π
                  %mo :
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi R
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi: c/R \to D
            that forgets the morphism
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi d
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L c = \lim_{c\to R d} \pi
                  \\,.
          %p
            Because with this definition there would be, for every
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi R
                  %mi d
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mi d
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi d
                      %mo ′
                  %mi d
                  %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L R d \stackrel{=}{\to} \lim_{R d \to R d'} d' \to d
          %p
            (the component map over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            of the limiting
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            preserves limits, we would have an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi L
                  %mi c
                  %mo ≃
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo →
                      %mi R
                      %mi d
                  %mi R
                  %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R L c \simeq \lim_{c\to R d} R d
          %p
            (the limit of the functor
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \pi
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cone"} cone
            tips
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo →
                  %mi R
                  %mi L
                  %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  c \to R L c
                  \\,.
          %p
            It is easy to check that these would be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+an+adjunction"} counit
            of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\dashv R
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            for more.
          %p
            The problem with this would-be argument is that in general the comma category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c/G)
            may not be
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c/G)
            is considered small, a
            %a{:href => "complete+small+category#CompleteSmallCategoriesArePosets"} classical theorem of Freyd
            says that any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+small+category"} complete small category
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
            (see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+small+category"} complete small category
            for the proof, which is valid in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            and also holds classically in any
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong adjoint functor theorem for preorders
          #ForPosets.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi D
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:D\to C
              is any functor between (small) preorders such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              has, and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              preserves, all small
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              has a left adjoint.
          %p
            (This theorem holds in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive mathematics
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+mathematics"} predicative mathematics
          %p
            To obtain adjoint functor theorems for categories that are not preorders, one must therefore impose various additional “size conditions” on the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            and/or the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h2#statement Statement
          %h3#general General
          #StandardAdjointFunctorTheorem.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              Sufficient conditions for a limit-preserving functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \colon C \to D
              to be a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              include:
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  is
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complete+category"} complete
                  and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
                  satisfies the
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solution+set+condition"} solution set condition
                %p
                  This is Freyd’s original version, sometimes called the “
                  %strong> General Adjoint Functor Theorem
                  ”.
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  is complete, locally small,
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/well-powered+category"} well-powered
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cogenerating+set"} cogenerating set
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                  is
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small
                %p
                  This is sometimes called the “
                  %strong> Special Adjoint Functor Theorem
                  ”, and abbreviated to SAFT.
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                  is locally small and
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+category"} cototal
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                  is locally small.
          %p
            In the first two cases, which work by replacing large limits by small ones, it suffices to assume that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            preserves small limits (that it preserves all limits will follow). The third case works by assuming that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            has, while not all large limits, enough so that the theorem goes through; thus is this case
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            must be already known to preserve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"} large
            limits as well.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              That a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \colon C \to D
              out of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally small category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with all small limits has a left adjoint if it preserves these
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solution+set+condition"} solution set condition
            %p
              From the discussion at
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/adjoint%20functor#UniversalArrows"} adjoint functors – In terms of universal arrows
              we have that the existence of the adjoint is equivalent to the existence for each
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in D
              of an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+object"} initial object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mi d
                    %mo :
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi L
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_d : d \to R L d
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+category"} comma category
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (d \downarrow R)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi d
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                    %mi d
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : d \to R d'
              there is a unique
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde f
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi d
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mrow
                              %mpadded{:lspace => "-100%width", :width => "0"}
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi i
                                    %mi d
                            %mo ↙
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mo ↘
                              %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                %mi f
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                            %mi L
                            %mi d
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi R
                                %mover
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi R
                            %mi d
                            %mo ′
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi L
                            %mi d
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mover
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi d
                            %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    \&amp;&amp; d
                    \\\
                    \&amp; {}^{\mathllap{i_d}}\swarrow &amp;&amp; \searrow^{\mathrlap{f}}
                    \\\
                    R L d &amp;&amp;\underset{R \tilde f}{\to}&amp;&amp; R d'
                    \\\
                    \\\
                    L d &amp;&amp;\underset{\tilde f}{\to}&amp;&amp; d'
                    }
            %p commutes. Now an initial object is the limit of the identity functor, but this is generally a large limit; we replace this with some small limit conditions that guarantee existence of an initial object.
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  Let
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  be a category. Call a small family of objects
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  %em weakly initial
                  if for every object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                  of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  there exists
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in F
                  and a morphism
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo :
                          %mi x
                          %mo →
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: x \to y
              %li
                %p
                  Suppose
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  has small products. If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  is a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+multilimit"} weakly initial family
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                          %mrow
                            %mi x
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi F
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prod_{x \in F} x
                  is a
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakly+initial+object"} weakly initial object
              %li
                %p
                  %strong Claim:
                  Suppose
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                  is locally small and has joint equalizers of small families. If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                  is a weakly initial object, then the domain
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                  of the joint equalizer
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo :
                        %mi e
                        %mo →
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: e \to x
                  of all arrows
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo →
                        %mi x
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to x
                  is an initial object.
                  %strong Proof:
                  clearly
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
                  is weakly initial. Suppose given an object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
                  and arrows
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo ,
                          %mi g
                          %mo :
                          %mi e
                          %mo →
                          %mi y
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f, g: e \to y
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi f
                          %mo =
                          %mi g
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = g
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi j
                        %mo :
                        %mi d
                        %mo →
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j: d \to e
                  be the equalizer of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                  and
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
