          %h3#StatementForLocPresCategories For locally presentable categories
          %p
            In practice an important special case is that of functors between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
          #AdjFuncTheoremForLocallyPresentableCats.num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon C \to D
              be a functor between
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  has a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
                  if and only if it preserves all small
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                  has a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoint"} left adjoint
                  if and only if it is an
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/accessible+functor"} accessible functor
                  and preserves all small
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
          %p
            The second statement, characterizing when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            has a left adjoint, is (
            %a{:href => "#AdamekRosicky"}> AdamekRosicky, theorem 1.66
            ). In the “if” direction, this is an application of the general adjoint functor theorem: any accessible functor satisfies the solution set condition. The “only if”, particularly that having a left adjoint forces accessibility, takes a little work. But in any case there are easy examples that show that continuity alone is insufficient, i.e., examples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functors"} continuous functors
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
            that do not have
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+adjoints"} left adjoints
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#InLocallyPresentableCategories"} In locally presentable categories
          %p
            The first statement, characterizing when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            has a right adjoint, can be proven using the special adjoint functor theorem: by a non-trivial theorem (
            %a{:href => "#AdamekRosicky"}> AdamekRosicky, theorem 1.58
            ), any locally presentable category is co-wellpowered.
          %p
            Thus, the first statement can be strengthened by removing the assumption that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is locally presentable: it is enough that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be locally small. For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is locally presentable, then every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functor"} continuous functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to Set
          %p
            has a left adjoint (is representable), because its opposite
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to Set^{op}
            is cocontinuous and therefore has a right adjoint, even though
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
