          %h3#InPresheafCategories In presheaf categories
          %p
            It is instructive to spell out the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            from a colimit preserving functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            in the simple case where all categories are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+presheaves"} categories of presheaves
          %p
            So let now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+categories"} small categories
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            a
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+colimit"} preserving
            functor between their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+presheaves"} categories of presheaves
            (which we abbreviate
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi C
                      %mo ^
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ≔
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat C \;\coloneqq\; [C^{op}, Set]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo ^
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L \;\colon\; \widehat{C} \longrightarrow \widehat{D}
          %p
            Then its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo ^
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R \;\colon\; \widehat{D} \longrightarrow \widehat{C}
            is given (with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi c
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y_c \coloneqq C(-, c)
            denoting the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
          #eq:RightAdjointOfColimitPreservingFunctorOfPresheaves.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↦
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R
                  \\;\colon\;
                  A
                  \\;\mapsto\;
                  R(A)
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  \\widehat{D}
                  \\left(
                  L(y_{(-)}),
                  A
                  \\right)
                  \\,,
          %p
            as we shall check in a moment. But first notice that using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Yoneda+lemma"} co-Yoneda lemma
            this may be rewritten as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \\;\simeq\;
                  \\int^{c \in C}
                  \\widehat{D}
                  \\left(
                  L(y_c), A
                  \\right)
                  \\cdot y_c
                  \\,,
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            is equivalently given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimit"} colimit
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %munder
                    %mi lim
                    %munder
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi L
                        %mi c
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                  %mi c
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots \;=\; \lim_{\underset{L c \to A}{\to}} c
                  \\,.
          %p This is the formula for the would-be right adjoint from the general discussion above, only that here the colimit is only over the representables, hence over a small category.
          %p
            Now we check that the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            thus obtained is indeed right adjoint to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "adjoint+functor#InTermsOfHomIsomorphism"} here
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functors"} adjoint functors
          %p
            We compute
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi D
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat{D}(L(X),A)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mo ∫
                        %mrow
                          %mi c
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi C
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⋅
                      %msub
                        %mi y
                        %mi c
                      %mo )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat{D}(L(X), A)
                  \\simeq
                  \\widehat{D}
                  \\left(
                  L
                  \\left(
                  \\int^{c \in C} X(c) \cdot y_c
                  \\right),
                  A
                  \\right)
          %p
            we use the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Yoneda+lemma"} co-Yoneda lemma
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preserved+limit"} preserves
            colimits, this is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ≃
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi c
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \\simeq
                  \\widehat{D}
                  \\left(
                  \\int^c X(c) \cdot L(y_c), A
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
            preserves limits in both arguments, we can take the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
            out to get an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/end"} end
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi y
                    %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \\simeq
                  \\int_{c \in C}
                  \\widehat{D}(X(c) \cdot L(y_c), A)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then we use the standard
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensoring
            of our categories over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            to get
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                  %mi Set
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi D
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \\simeq
                  \\int_{c \in C}
                  Set\left(
                  X(c),
                  \\widehat{D}(L(y_c),A)
                  \\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            And finally this is recognized as the formula for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-set
            of presheaves (see the discussion at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor+category"} functor category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ≃
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo ^
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mi D
                      %mo ^
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi y
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\cdots
                  \\simeq
                  \\widehat{C}
                  \\left(
                  X,
                  \\widehat{D}(L(y_{(-)}),A)
                  \\right)
                  \\;=\;
                  \\widehat{C}(X, R A)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where on the right we identified
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:RightAdjointOfColimitPreservingFunctorOfPresheaves"} (1)
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
            of this sequence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+isomorphisms"} natural isomorphisms
            is hence the desired hom-isomorphism:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi D
                    %mo ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mover
                    %mi C
                    %mo ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat{D}(L(X), A) \simeq \widehat{C}(X, R(A))
                  \\,.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocontinuous+functor"}> co
                )
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functor"} continuous functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+adjoint+functor+theorem"} indexed adjoint functor theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor+theorem"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+triangle+theorem"} adjoint triangle theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+lifting+theorem"} adjoint lifting theorem
          %h2#references References
          %p The classical adjoint functor theorems originate in the exercise section of ch.3 (pp.84ff) in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Abelian Categories
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.tac.mta.ca/tac/reprints/articles/3/tr3abs.html"} TAC reprints no. 3 (2003)
          %p A more recent exposition is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Freyd"}> Peter Freyd
              , Andre Scedrov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Categories, Allegories
          %p
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solution+set+condition"} solution set condition
            is called “pre-adjointness”.
          %p Careful discussions can be found in
          %ul
            %li#Bouceux
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Handbook+of+Categorical+Algebra"} Handbook of Categorical Algebra
                    I
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                , §V.6, V.8 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                %strong 5
                Springer (1971, second ed. 1997) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            A brief introductory discussion is around
            %a{:href => "http://www.staff.science.uu.nl/~ooste110/syllabi/catsmoeder.pdf#page=54"} theorem 5.4
            of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jaap+van+Oosten"}> Jaap van Oosten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Basic category theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.staff.science.uu.nl/~ooste110/syllabi/catsmoeder.pdf"} pdf
          %p A detailed expository survey is
          %ul
            %li
              Oliver Kullmann,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The adjoint functor theorem
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cs.swan.ac.uk/~csoliver/Hauptseminar/Quellen/200702Swansea_b.pdf"} pdf slides
          %p
            The adjoint functor theorem in context with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+embedding"} Yoneda embedding
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Friedrich Ulmer,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The adjoint functor theorem and the Yoneda embedding
              %strong 15
              no.3 (1971), pp. 355-361. (
              %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ijm/1256052605"}> euclid
              )
          %p This perspective is extended to explore a spectrum of adjoint functor theorems in:
          %ul
            %li#ALL23
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathanael+Arkor"}> Nathanael Arkor
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ivan+Di+Liberti"} Ivan Di Liberti
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fosco+Loregian"} Fosco Loregian
              = succeed "," do
                %em Adjoint functor theorems for lax-idempotent pseudomonads
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.10389"} arXiv:2306.10389
          %p The connection between the solution set condition and the Čech homology construction is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              Renato Betti,
              %em Čech methods and the adjoint functor theorem
              , Cah. Top. Géom. Diff. Cat.
              %strong XXVI
              no.3 (1985) pp.245-257. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=CTGDC_1985__26_3_245_0"}> numdam
              )
          %p An enriched adjoint functor theorem is given in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
              \.
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  Limites enrichies et existence de
                  = succeed "-foncteur" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi V
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1975__16_4_395_0.pdf"} pdf
          %p The parallels between the adjoint functor theorem for categories and the computation of colimits from limits in a lattice as well as similar parallels between co/completeness for Boolean algebras and Paré’s theorem for finite completeness of toposes is studied from a type-theoretical perspective in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dusko+Pavlovic"}> Duško Pavlović
              ,
              %em On completeness and cocompleteness in and around small categories
              , APAL
              %strong 74
              (1995) pp.121-152.
          %p
            The case for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+presentable+categories"} locally presentable categories
            is discussed in
          %ul#AdamekKoubekTrnkova
            %li
              %p#AdamekRosicky
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jiri+Rosicky"} Jiri Rosicky
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Locally+presentable+and+accessible+categories"} Locally presentable and accessible categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
                , V. Koubek and V. Trnková,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em How large are left exact functors?
                %strong 8
                (2001), pp. 377-390. (
                %a{:href => "http://www.emis.de/journals/TAC/volumes/8/n13/8-13abs.html"}> abstract
                )
          %p
            A specialization to the locally
            = succeed "-presentable" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Giacomo+Tendas"}> Giacomo Tendas
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On continuity of accessible functors
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s10485-022-09677-x"} doi
          %p A relative version of Freyd’s classical results is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"}> Brian Day
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An adjoint-functor theorem over topoi
              %strong 15
              (1976), pp. 381-394.
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/indexed+adjoint+functor+theorem"} Adjoint functor theorems for indexed categories
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Par%C3%A9"}> Robert Paré
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dietmar+Schumacher"} Dietmar Schumacher
              %em Abstract Families and the Adjoint Functor Theorems
              , pp.1-125 in LNM
              %strong 661
              Springer Heidelberg 1978.
          %p A history of the general adjoint functor theorem is discussed in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans-E.+Porst"}> Hans-E. Porst
              ,
              %em The history of the General Adjoint Functor Theorem
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.19528"}> arxiv:2310.19528
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p A stronger version for finitary functors between locally finitely presentable categories whose domain is ranked, requiring only the preservation of countable limits for the existence of a left adjoint, is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ji%C5%99%C3%AD+Ad%C3%A1mek"}> Jiří Adámek
              , Lurdes Sousa,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Finitary Adjoint Functor Theorem
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.14965"} arXiv
        .revisedby
